      "content": "A changelog generator first parses each commit message, typically following a convention such as Conventional Commits. It extracts the type (e.g., `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`), an optional scope (e.g., `auth`, `ui`), and any breaking‑change flag. By normalizing these fields, the tool can automatically map each commit to a semantic bucket that will later become a release‑note section.\n\nNext, the generator groups the parsed commits by their type or scope, optionally ordering them by priority (breaking changes first, then features, fixes, docs, etc.). Within each group it can sort commits chronologically or alphabetically, and collapse duplicate messages that share the same scope. This grouping logic can be customized via configuration files or command‑line flags, allowing teams to decide whether to split by component, by commit type, or by a combination of both.\n\nFinally, the grouped data is rendered into a human‑readable format—Markdown, HTML, or plain text—using templates. Each section header (e.g., “### Features”, “### Bug Fixes”) is followed by a bullet list of commit summaries, optionally including links to the commit hashes or pull requests. The generator can also inject a “Breaking Changes” section if any commit is marked as such, ensuring that release notes are both structured and informative.",
