      "content": "A changelog generator first parses each commit message, typically following a convention such as Conventional Commits.  It extracts the type (e.g., `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`), an optional scope (e.g., `auth`, `ui`), and any breaking‑change flag.  By mapping these tokens to a predefined hierarchy, the tool can automatically sort commits into logical buckets—“New Features”, “Bug Fixes”, “Documentation”, “Performance Improvements”, etc.—and further subdivide them by scope if desired.\n\nOnce the commits are bucketed, the generator orders the sections by importance or release strategy.  For example, breaking changes are highlighted first, followed by features, then fixes, and finally docs or chores.  Within each section, commits can be sorted chronologically or by the number of contributors, and each entry can be rendered as a concise bullet point that includes the commit hash, a short subject line, and optionally a link to the full diff.\n\nFinally, the tool compiles the grouped sections into a release‑notes template (Markdown, HTML, or plain text).  It can inject metadata such as the release version, date, and a changelog header, and optionally merge duplicate entries or collapse minor changes into a “Miscellaneous” section.  The resulting document is ready for inclusion in a release announcement, ensuring that stakeholders see a clear, organized summary of what changed in each version.",
